Deciding that the original icon is that of विष्णु and not that of a Śaivite deity;
Decorating the two upper hands with शङ्ख (conch) and चक्र (discus), the chief symbols of विष्णु;
Fixing a symbol of लक्ष्मी (called here as Alarmelmaṅgai) on the chest of the icon;
Establishing the images of Varāha (Boar-इन्चर्नतिओन्), नरसिṁह (Lion-इन्चर्नतिओन्), Vaikuṇṭhanātha and वेङ्कटेश (two aspects of विष्णु) on all the four corners of the विमान (tower) above the गर्भगृह (sanctum);
Stationing the dvārapālakas (gate-keepers) and other images as of अनन्त (serpent-bed of विष्णु), गरुड (eagle-mount of विष्णु) and others in their respective positions;
Applying the ऊर्ध्वपुण्ड्र (commonly known as nāma, the religious symbol of the Śrīvaiṣṇavas, followers of Rāmānuja) on the forehead of the main image;
Selecting the image for processional purposes (the utsvamūrti), which is being used even now;
Building up the तिरुपति township just below the hill, wherein the main temple staff would live, as also the Govindarāja temple.
